 ### Barycentric Color Interpolation for Voxels
-Interpolates vertex colors to a voxel center by treating the voxel center as a point inside the triangle and weighting the vertex colors by the areas of the sub-triangles formed with the voxel center [S7, unofficial]. The interpolated color $c$ is calculated as:
-$$ c = c_1 \frac{A_2}{A} + c_2 \frac{A_3}{A} + c_3 \frac{A_1}{A} $$
-where $A_1 = \text{area}(v_1, v_2, P)$, $A_2 = \text{area}(v_2, v_3, P)$, $A_3 = \text{area}(v_3, v_1, P)$, $A = A_1 + A_2 + A_3$, and $P$ is the voxel center [S7, unofficial].
+Interpolates vertex colors to a voxel center by treating the voxel center as a point inside the triangle and weighting the vertex colors by the areas of the sub-triangles formed with the voxel center. The interpolated color $c$ is calculated as $c = c_1 \frac{A_2}{A} + c_2 \frac{A_3}{A} + c_3 \frac{A_1}{A}$, where $A_1 = \text{area}(v_1, v_2, P)$, $A_2 = \text{area}(v_2, v_3, P)$, $A_3 = \text{area}(v_3, v_1, P)$, $A = A_1 + A_2 + A_3$, and $P$ is the voxel center [S7, unofficial].
 
 ### Adaptive Interpolation for Isosurface Detection
-Interpolates input volume along rays at a predetermined sampling rate. It uses non-binary classifiers to adjust the acceptable density range for an isovalue surface based on the data change rate [S3, unofficial]. When data values change slowly, a narrow range of acceptable values is used for the isovalue surface; when data values change rapidly, a wide range is used to avoid missing the surface [S3, unofficial]. This method supports user selection of interpolation: nearest-neighbor (preview), linear (day-to-day), or cubic (publication-quality) [S3, unofficial].
