 ## Background
-The problem of interpolating discrete voxel data for volume rendering has been studied since the early days of the field. Levoy introduced ray-casting methods in 1988, noting that simple binary classifiers could miss isosurfaces if sampling rates were insufficient, leading to the adoption of interpolation [S3, unofficial]. Westover's 1990 work on "Footprint evaluation" formalized the use of reconstruction filters (interpolation kernels) to determine the contribution of voxels to a pixel, highlighting the artifacts of nearest-neighbor interpolation [S4, S5, unofficial].
+The study of interpolation in volume rendering emerged alongside the development of direct volume rendering techniques in the late 1980s and early 1990s. Early work by Levoy and Westover highlighted the visual artifacts introduced by simple sampling methods [S3, S4, unofficial]. Nearest-neighbor interpolation, often referred to as zero-order interpolation or sample-and-hold, was identified as the simplest but most artifact-prone method, producing "staircase" effects and blockiness [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. Trilinear interpolation was introduced as the first-order improvement, eliminating the sharp discontinuities of nearest-neighbor at the cost of increased computation [S1, S4, unofficial].
 
-Nearest-neighbor interpolation, also known as zero-order interpolation or sample-and-hold, is the simplest method, assigning the value of the closest voxel [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It is equivalent to convolving the signal with a rect function (box filter) [S3, unofficial]. While computationally efficient (requiring only 1 sample in 3D), it produces severe staircase artifacts and blockiness due to its poor low-pass filtering characteristics (distorted passband and stopband leakage) [S3, S4, unofficial].
+Higher-order methods, such as tri-cubic B-spline and windowed sinc filters, were developed to provide smoother, higher-quality images suitable for publication and detailed analysis [S4, S5, unofficial]. The sinc filter, theoretically ideal for reconstruction, requires infinite support, leading to the development of windowed sinc filters (e.g., Blackman window) and efficient approximations like the fast tri-cubic B-spline method [S4, S5, unofficial][S8].
 
-Trilinear interpolation (first-order) was introduced to eliminate these artifacts by assuming linear relationships between neighbors [S1, S4, unofficial]. It requires 8 samples in 3D and provides $C^0$ continuity but suffers from discontinuous first derivatives, leading to a "star pattern" artifact in enlarged images [S3, unofficial].
-
-Higher-order schemes, such as tri-cubic interpolation using B-splines or windowed sinc filters, were developed to improve quality further [S4, S5, unofficial]. The windowed sinc filter, particularly with a Blackman window of width four, is cited as providing high-quality results by better approximating the ideal reconstruction filter [S4, S5, unofficial].
-
-Frequency domain volume rendering, introduced by Westover in 1993, offered a novel approach by performing resampling in the Fourier domain to avoid the blurring inherent in spatial domain interpolation [S5, unofficial]. This method became particularly relevant for large zoom factors and oblique projections where spatial interpolation artifacts (ghosting) are prominent [S8].
+Frequency domain volume rendering, introduced by Westover and later optimized for GPUs, shifted the resampling process from the spatial to the frequency domain to leverage the properties of the Fourier transform, particularly for handling large zoom factors and shearing without blurring [S1, S5, unofficial]. This approach relies on the ideal sinc filter's properties in the frequency domain, implemented via zero-padding and FFTs [S1, unofficial][S8].
 
